{
 "id": "1876-fechner-golden",
 "name": "Golden section rectangles – which shape pleases most?",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the first experiment in empirical aesthetics: Gustav Theodor Fechner's rectangle test (Vorschule der Aesthetik, 1876, Part 1, Chapter XIV, pp. 193–196). Ten white rectangles of equal area, with side ratios 1:1, 5:6, 4:5, 3:4, 20:29, 2:3, 21:34 (golden section), 13:23, 1:2 and 2:5, lie at random angles in random order on a black board; participants choose the most pleasing and the least pleasing one, without thinking of any particular use. Fechner collected 347 preference judgements (228 men, 119 women) and found the golden-section rectangle chosen most often. Stimuli are redrawn in the page from the ratios and equal area given in the book (public domain); each answer stores the layout shown. Differences from the original study: (1) shapes on screen instead of white card on a black table; (2) one forced choice each instead of allowing hesitation between two or three rectangles (Fechner counted those as 0.5 or 0.33); (3) questions on age and art training added.",
